"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.3. The surgical findings included a pathological stage of pT3a with extraprostatic extension and positive surgical margins, which are factors suggesting higher risk. Conversely, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+3) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 3+4). The absence of lymph node removal and the lack of nodal sampling introduce uncertainty, as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The primary driver for prognosis is the positive margins and T3a stage. Information that would revise this prognosis significantly includes the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data currently limits the certainty of the recurrence risk assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 56.82 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 36.93–76.70 months; its coverage has not been validated."
